  the tab is open. If "notified" in your head means email or a phone buzz, that requires the server, and we should
  choose it now rather than discover it later.

[38;5;246m⏺ [39mUser answered Claude's questions:
[38;5;246m  ⎿  · Where should task data live? → Browser-only, behind a seam (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Next: what a task actually is. This matters more than it looks, because a notification is about a [3mtransition[0m — so the
  fields you have are exactly the set of things that can change.

  [1mThe fork:[0m how much structure a task carries. With title + done only, there is precisely one transition in the whole
  system ("marked done"), and the notifications subsystem has almost nothing to say.

  - [1mMinimal[0m — title, done. Buys: trivial to build. Costs: one notifiable event; notifications become a feature with
    nothing to report.
  - [1mModerate[0m — title, notes, status (todo/doing/done), due date, priority. Buys: several genuinely distinct transitions
    — status moved, due date changed, due date approaching. Enough for notifications to be a real subsystem. Costs: more
    UI.
  - [1mRich[0m — the above plus tags, subtasks, attachments, comments. Buys: comments in particular are a strong notification
    source. Costs: substantially more app to build before we get to what you asked for.

  [1mMy recommendation: moderate.[0m It's the smallest task model where "notify me when tasks I care about change" is a
  sentence with real content behind it. Minimal would make the notifications cycle feel pointless; rich delays it.
